      ["LeanFM/Session/Where.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Conform.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Skeleton.lean", "LeanFM/Session/SkeletonTs.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Diagram.lean"]
      "LeanFM.Session.Where parses a condition's text (Guard.text, the same in the text form and the Lean form) into a closed Expr by recursive descent (or, and, not, comparison, sum, product, unary minus, atoms) and evaluates it with an interpreter over Value (num Rat, str, bool): there is no constructor holding code. Where.eval takes a lookup from a message id to its event's fields (dotted keys) and answers .yes, .no or .unknown with a reason; comparison of different types, a missing field, an unbound id and division by zero are .unknown. Where.paths lists (id, field path) pairs a condition reads. LogEvent gains fields, parsed by SessionTools.logEvent from the event's fields object, nested objects flattened to dotted keys, numbers as exact rationals, strings and booleans kept, others dropped; at most 64 fields, keys and strings within 200 characters. Conform.step evaluates the guard of a matching message with the session's bindings plus the current event: .no drops that transition, .unknown keeps it and records an unchecked entry carried in the state (outside its key); checkSession reports, when no state survives an event and some transition was dropped only by its guard, the condition and the values it read, and returns the unchecked entries of a surviving state. session_conform adds unchecked per session and their count to the reading. Text.parse refuses a condition Where cannot parse or Types cannot type (decision session-types). Skeleton and SkeletonTs give each send the list of fields conditions read for its message id (from Where.paths over every guard) and log fields: the body projected to exactly those paths. Diagram.draw, for a choice, takes the guard of each branch's first message as that operand's guard: under the frame tab for the first operand, on the divider for the others, and draws that message without it."
